The C quadtree library was translated to Rust. The project implements a spatial quadtree data structure with:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mQuadtreePoint[0m — simple x/y coordinate pair[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mQuadtreeBounds[0m — axis-aligned bounding box with NW/SE corners, width, and height[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mQuadtreeNode<T>[0m — tree node with 4 children (NE/NW/SE/SW), bounds, an optional point, and a generic key[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mQuadtree<T>[0m — the tree itself with a root node, length counter, and optional key-free callback[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mKey implementation details:[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- The Rust signatures use [38;5;10m&self[0m for several methods that need mutation (matching the C API's pointer-based approach). I used [38;5;10mptr::from_ref()[0m + raw pointer dereference ([38;5;10munsafe[0m) to handle this, since the struct definitions couldn't be changed to use [38;5;10mUnsafeCell[0m/[38;5;10mRefCell[0m.[0m[0m
- The actual tree logic (insert, search, split, walk) is implemented in private module-level helper functions ([38;5;10minsert_impl[0m, [38;5;10mfind_impl[0m, [38;5;10msplit_node_mut[0m, [38;5;10mwalk_impl[0m, etc.) that take [38;5;10m&mut[0m references properly.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mquadtree_search[0m returns [38;5;10m&mut Option<Box<QuadtreePoint>>[0m — for the [38;5;10mNone[0m case, a [38;5;10mstatic mut[0m is used as a stable location.[0m[0m
- Insert returns [38;5;10mbool[0m (the C version returns int with 0=fail, 1=normal, 2=replace). The Rust version returns [38;5;10mtrue[0m for any successful insert and increments [38;5;10mlength[0m only on new insertions (status 1).[0m[0m
- Memory management ([38;5;10m_free[0m methods) are no-ops since Rust's ownership system handles deallocation via [38;5;10mDrop[0m.[0m[0m[38;5;12m[1m✓ Created checkpoint 1

